out 96% to win", "District's modest Democratic lean plus incumbency advantage"], "rationale": "Every available signal points the same direction: all ten expert raters call the race Safe or Solid D, markets price Davids near 96%, and she has raised $3.7 million against about $19,000 for Jenkins with no outside spending on either side. With no public polling, the forecast rests on fundamentals, but the incumbent's cash edge, bipartisan legislative record, and the district's Democratic lean justify a Safe D rating.", "watch": "A late influx of outside spending against Davids or a sharp national shift toward Republicans could narrow what is currently a low-attention race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Sharice Davids (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Sharice Davids seeks a fifth term in a suburban Kansas City district that leans slightly Democratic. She won her primary easily in August and will face Republican Eric Jenkins, a little-funded challenger, plus a Libertarian candidate in November. No public general-election polling is available for this race.\n\nThe evidence overwhelmingly favors Davids. All ten expert ratings place the race in the Safe or Solid Democratic column, and prediction markets give her roughly a 96 percent chance of winning. She has raised about $3.7 million and holds $1.7 million on hand, while Jenkins has raised under $20,000, and no outside spending has been reported. Her recent headlines emphasize healthcare cost legislation and bipartisan work, including a bill co-sponsored with a Republican. The main uncertainty is the absence of polling, which keeps uncertainty moderately wide despite the lopsided fundamentals.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9826, "margin": 15.09}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9781, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9789, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.979, "margin"